We login as usual with the password gained from the previous level.
    root@ubuntu:~$ ssh bandit2@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

The next password is stored in a file called --spaces in this filename-- located in the home directory.

We use the same method as the previous level to gain the password. We can start entering the name of the file and press tab to finish.
        bandit2@bandit:~$ ls
        --spaces in this filename--
        bandit2@bandit:~$ cat ./--spaces\ in\ this\ filename-- 
        7ZZ2LFrykP2zEyvBl4m3clcL7tGYJPME

As this file has spaces in the name, we use \ to mark the spaces.
If we don't it will treat every new word as a seperate file. e.g
        bandit2@bandit:~$ cat ./--spaces in this filename-- 
        cat: ./--spaces: No such file or directory
        cat: in: No such file or directory
        cat: this: No such file or directory
        cat: filename--: No such file or directory

We can also use "" to deal with spaces.
        bandit2@bandit:~$ cat ./"--spaces in this filename--"
        7ZZ2LFrykP2zEyvBl4m3clcL7tGYJPME

The password is 7ZZ2LFrykP2zEyvBl4m3clcL7tGYJPME
